! conclusion:
  The choice of the next action is accountable when it can say, with
  evidence: what was chosen, from what candidates, under what
  preference, at what uncertainty — and the preference was RECEIVED
  from where preference legitimately lives, never computed by the
  chooser.

  + context:
    Selection machinery exists (ranked-actions, intrinsic-value
    posteriors, preregistered G); goal formation is the harder half
    (pre-goal preferences, C_ser); and the failure mode is a chooser
    that quietly becomes the source of the preferences it optimizes.

  + IF:
    Any agent, loop, or manager selects what work happens next.

  + HOWEVER:
    Under real uncertainty the account includes the uncertainty:
    wide intervals, unranked ties, and "no action cleared its
    threshold" are all legal typed outcomes — accountable does not
    mean confident. Simulated demand (Part III agents) is input to
    preference, never preference itself.

  + THEN:
    The selector emits its account with the choice: candidates,
    ranked-actions with reasons, the received preference's
    provenance (operator pin or rehydrated posterior), the
    uncertainty typed, and the shared-budget arithmetic. Choosing
    without emitting the account is the violation.

  + BECAUSE:
    "Accountable next-action choice" is the apex thesis's D stated
    as a constraint; the constitutional line (priority received,
  never computed) is what keeps the crew a tool of its operator
    rather than an agent with opinions about what matters.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[The task-manager spec's constitutional line is this pattern's first enforcement; keep it verbatim when built.]
    next[Ranked-actions' missing reconciliation exits (lint ticket) are this pattern's open gap.]
